1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,970
A hundred and forty-two points: two averages, two standard deviations, and a correlation of minus point oh six.

2
00:00:08,350 --> 00:00:11,700
Which way does their best-fit line tilt, and how steeply?

3
00:00:13,533 --> 00:00:18,313
A shapeless oval cloud with a nearly flat line is the reasonable guess.

4
00:00:18,693 --> 00:00:25,003
Here are the real points: a dinosaur, drawn by Alberto Cairo, with these same five numbers.

5
00:00:25,666 --> 00:00:34,386
In a review of seven hundred and three physiology papers, eighty-six percent used bar graphs: an average and an error bar.

6
00:00:34,766 --> 00:00:37,026
Only thirteen percent showed the points.

7
00:00:37,366 --> 00:00:39,506
So how much can five numbers hide?

8
00:00:39,886 --> 00:00:45,696
Let's hide a star in them, and demand that all five still match the dinosaur's to two decimals.

9
00:00:45,900 --> 00:00:50,020
Version one slides every point onto its own spot on a star.

10
00:00:50,366 --> 00:00:53,296
We got the star, and lost all five numbers.

11
00:00:53,496 --> 00:00:58,806
The center moved, the x spread grew, and the y spread shrank by almost a quarter.

12
00:00:59,466 --> 00:01:02,156
So what exactly do five numbers pin down?

13
00:01:02,500 --> 00:01:06,670
The two averages mark a single point: the cloud's balance point.

14
00:01:06,833 --> 00:01:11,913
The two standard deviations draw a box, one deviation out on each side.

15
00:01:12,100 --> 00:01:14,390
Many ellipses touch all four walls.

16
00:01:14,590 --> 00:01:16,250
The correlation picks one.

17
00:01:16,630 --> 00:01:23,480
Correlation r means one standard deviation to the right, on average r standard deviations up.

18
00:01:23,860 --> 00:01:28,660
So the touch point on the right wall sits r times s y above the center.

19
00:01:29,040 --> 00:01:32,240
At minus point oh six, that's just below the middle.

20
00:01:32,600 --> 00:01:35,480
That ellipse is everything the five numbers know.

21
00:01:35,833 --> 00:01:38,753
Now replay version one, and watch the ellipse.

22
00:01:39,266 --> 00:01:40,896
How would you fix the star?

23
00:01:42,733 --> 00:01:44,693
Slide it until the centers meet.

24
00:01:45,073 --> 00:01:47,193
Stretch it until the boxes match.

25
00:01:47,573 --> 00:01:52,163
Then tip it, a slight shear, until it touches the walls at the same height.

26
00:01:52,500 --> 00:01:55,610
All five match now, to every decimal place.

27
00:01:56,266 --> 00:02:00,956
But add a sixth number, the median of y, and our star misses it by six.

28
00:02:01,300 --> 00:02:08,540
In 2017, Justin Matejka and George Fitzmaurice built a search that holds whatever numbers you choose.

29
00:02:08,690 --> 00:02:10,140
They chose these five.

30
00:02:10,333 --> 00:02:18,203
Nudge a point at random, biased toward a target shape, and keep the nudge only if the five numbers still hold to two decimals.

31
00:02:18,583 --> 00:02:22,223
Repeat it enough times, and the dinosaur becomes a circle.

32
00:02:22,566 --> 00:02:25,856
From the dinosaur they grew twelve shapes, like this star,

33
00:02:27,156 --> 00:02:28,216
this bullseye,

34
00:02:29,216 --> 00:02:30,086
this X,

35
00:02:31,286 --> 00:02:32,336
these stripes,

36
00:02:33,236 --> 00:02:34,336
and these dots.

37
00:02:34,716 --> 00:02:38,316
Every one of them has this same ellipse, and a different median.

38
00:02:39,033 --> 00:02:44,433
Francis Anscombe made the point in 1973, with eleven points of his own.

39
00:02:44,966 --> 00:02:49,756
His correlation is point eight two, so the ellipse touches the right wall high up.

40
00:02:50,100 --> 00:02:55,840
Least squares sets the slope of the best-fit line to the covariance over the x variance.

41
00:02:56,640 --> 00:03:00,170
The covariance is r times s x times s y,

42
00:03:01,170 --> 00:03:02,910
so one s x cancels.

43
00:03:03,366 --> 00:03:07,256
What's left is the rise to the touch point over the run to the wall.

44
00:03:07,456 --> 00:03:10,716
So the line runs through the center and that touch point.

45
00:03:11,096 --> 00:03:14,026
Plug in his numbers, and the slope is one half.

46
00:03:14,366 --> 00:03:19,566
All four of his datasets share these five numbers, so they share the ellipse and the line.

47
00:03:19,866 --> 00:03:23,966
A trend, a curve, an outlier, and one point doing all the work.

48
00:03:25,233 --> 00:03:28,643
Your line was exactly right: five numbers fix it.

49
00:03:29,023 --> 00:03:32,713
Your cloud was this ellipse, all that five numbers can draw.

50
00:03:33,313 --> 00:03:37,253
Most of those papers used a bar graph, which draws only two.

51
00:03:37,853 --> 00:03:40,553
The dinosaur lived in everything they leave out.

